"4/11/24, 10:51 AM (https://www.fordotosan.com.tr/documents/Documents/Diger_Duyurular/fitch-publishes-ford-otosans-bb-idr-outlook-stable-rates-proposed-eurobond-bbexp.pdf)\nciteturn18view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 1.4 years ago; Content type: application/pdf; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn13view0\",\"lineno\":92}); Redirected to URL: https://www.fordotosan.com.tr/documents/Documents/Diger_Duyurular/fitch-publishes-ford-otosans-bb-idr-outlook-stable-rates-proposed-eurobond-bbexp.pdf#page=1; Total lines: 320\nL0: 4/11/24, 10:51 AM\nL1: Fitch Publishes Ford Otosan's 'BB+' IDR; Outlook Stable; Rates Proposed Eurobond 'BB+(EXP)'\nL2: RATING ACTION COMMENTARY\nL3: Fitch Publishes Ford Otosan's 'BB+' IDR; Outlook Stable; Rates Proposed Eurobond 'BB+(EXP)'\nL4: Thu 11 Apr, 2024 - 4:43 AM ET\nL5: Fitch Ratings - Barcelona - 11 Apr 2024: Fitch Ratings has published Ford Otomotiv Sanayi A.S.'s (Ford Otosan) Long-Term Foreign-\nL6: Currency Issuer Default Rating (IDR) of 'BB+' with a Stable Outlook. Fitch has also assigned the company's proposed Eurobond an\nL7: expected senior unsecured rating of 'BB+(EXP)/RR4'. Final ratings are subject to the completion of refinancing and final documentation\nL8: confirming to information already received.\nL9: The ratings and Outlook consider Ford Otosan's solid through-the-cycle financial profile, which Fitch views as largely in line with\nL10: investment-grade automotive peers. The investment guarantee scheme embedded in the contract manufacturing agreement with Ford\nL11: Motor Company (FMC, BBB-/Stable) helps protect Ford Otosan's revenue and margins from a market downturn and ensures complete\nL12: capex recovery over the planned product cycle. We expect leverage metrics to closely follow the investment cycle and that the\nL13: deleveraging path after the initial investment spike will be supported by healthy free cash flow (FCF) generation before dividend\nL14: distribution.\nL15: Ford Otosan's business profile is characterised by its small scale and limited diversification compared with auto peers, which Fitch views\nL16: as constraining the rating. The nature of being a contract manufacturer implies little brand value although Ford-branded light\nL17: commercial vehicles (LCVs) produced by Ford Otosan have a leading market position in Europe. The internal synergy and ability to fund\nL18: R&D needs without tapping into FMC network is limited.\nL19: https://www.fitchratings.com/research/corporate-finance/fitch-publishes-ford-otosan-bb-idr-outlook-stable-rates-proposed-eurobond-bb-exp-11-04-2024\nL20: 1/13\nL21: 4/11/24, 10:51 AM\nL22: Fitch Publishes Ford Otosan's 'BB+' IDR; Outlook Stable; Rates Proposed Eurobond 'BB+(EXP)'\nL23: KEY RATING DRIVERS\nL24: Leading Europe Segment Market Share: Ford-branded commercial vehicles (CVs) have a leading position in Europe, which is expected\nL25: to grow together with new product launches. Transit, including its variants, is leading European van unit sales. This translated into Ford\nL26: Otosan's export revenues significantly increasing between 2015 and 2023. CV registration in Europe has rebounded strongly since the\nL27: start of 2023 from a low base in 2022, but it remains below the pre-pandemic level.\nL28: We expect demand for new CVs to remain strong as leisure and business services activities continue to recover, which will support high\nL29: capacity utilisation levels of 80-90% at Ford Otosan's plants.\nL30: Solid Through-the-Cycle Financial Profile: Ford Otosan's financial profile is commensurate with Fitch's expectation for low investment-\nL31: grade automakers. An EBIT margin between 5%-6% places it comfortably against the high investment-grade median (6%) in our rating\nL32: criteria for auto manufacturers. We forecast the FCF margins to remain negative to neutral over the rating horizon and EBITDA net\nL33: leverage to hover around 2x, driven by funding needs to roll out a four-year product pipeline. Ford Otosan has a record of healthy cash\nL34: flow generation, which gives the company deleveraging capacity once mass production starts.\nL35: Investment Guarantee Scheme: As part of the contract manufacturing agreement, FMC provides Ford Otosan with an investment\nL36: guarantee that secures a contractual volume despite actual sales volumes and enables Ford Otosan to recover upfront capex over the\nL37: planned product cycle. The scheme also entails a cost-plus pricing mechanism that incorporates the full pass-through of the production\nL38: expense and a profit mark-up. The investment guarantee scheme effectively serves as floor for Ford Otosan's revenue and earnings and\nL39: offers some protection from a market downturn, which was evident by the resilient performance throughout the pandemic.\nL40: Scale, Diversification Constrain Rating: Ford Otosan is considered small compared with European vehicle manufacturers including\nL41: Volkswagen AG and Mercedes-Benz Group AG that also feature a much broader spectrum of vehicle types, brands, and models and are\nL42: well-diversified geographically. With expected production capacity above 900,000 by 2025, Ford Otosan is among the top producers\nL43: when compared with the LCV/van segments of Renault, Stellantis N.V., and VW.\nL44: Consequently, we deem Ford Otosan a niche player despite its leading position in the covered markets and its size and scale constrain\nL45: the rating at the current level. Additionally, Ford Otosan's engineering know-how and own intellectual property rights are largely\nL46: https://www.fitchratings.com/research/corporate-finance/fitch-publishes-ford-otosan-bb-idr-outlook-stable-rates-proposed-eurobond-bb-exp-11-04-2024\nL47: 2/13\nL48: 4/11/24, 10:51 AM\nL49: Fitch Publishes Ford Otosan's 'BB+' IDR; Outlook Stable; Rates Proposed Eurobond 'BB+(EXP)'\nL50: associated with Ford branded trucks that do not have a meaningful market share outside Turkiye.\nL51: Appropriate Funding Structure: Around 10% of Ford Otosan's capital structure relies on short-term debt financing. This is common for\nL52: Turkish corporates. We expect short-term bank lines will remain available. Ford Otosan has also access to factoring and trade lines that\nL53: are not utilised. Its largest customer is Ford Deutschland Holding GmbH, which it derives around 80% of its annual sales, keeping\nL54: receivable turnover to 14 days in Turkiye and 30 days in Romania. The high hard currency (HC) receivable collection rates support euro-\nL55: denominated interest payments on its international borrowings, mitigating FX risks.\nL56: Margin Upside from Domestic Sales: Sales to the domestic market where Ford Otosan has discretion over pricing and distribution\nL57: channels improve its profitability (EBITDA margin) although domestic unit sales have been volatile yoy and can contract fairly quickly in\nL58: a worsening operating environment. Ford brands make up around 9% of the Turkish auto market share with a leading position in CVs.\nL59: The passenger car (PC) market remains competitive and will remain dominated by Fiat and Renault, in our view. Despite the good\nL60: margins from domestic sales, we expect group profitability to be lower as exports represent around 80% of sales.\nL61: Strategic Importance for Ford: Fitch believes that Ford Otosan is strategically important for its joint venture (JV) parent FMC,\nL62: producing about two-thirds of Ford's CV unit sales and one-third of PCs in Europe and provides material cost advantage largely due to\nL63: cheaper labour. Ford Otosan is likely to gain more importance while FMC is ramping down its manufacturing sites in Europe. Ford\nL64: Otosan also plays a pivotal role in FMC's global CV strategy and electrification roadmap. It will manufacture six out of nine FMC's future\nL65: electric models, including E-Transit, E-Custom, and the flagship LCVs is set to be a key pillar of its electric vehicle arm.\nL66: Rating Reflects Standalone Profile: As a JV, Ford Otosan's IDR currently reflects its Standalone Credit Profile (SCP). Fitch views the\nL67: links between FMC and Ford Otosan as potentially supporting a one-notch rating uplift from Ford Otosan's SCP. This reflects our\nL68: assessment using Parent and Subsidiary Linkage Rating Criteria (PSL) where we see operational and strategic incentives for FMC to\nL69: support Ford Otosan, offset by the lack of debt guarantees or cross-default clauses.\nL70: We have not applied any additional notching to Ford Otosan's IDR under the PSL as this would lead to rating equalisation with FMC,\nL71: which we do not deem appropriate due to the existing JV structure and proximity of credit profiles. The rating uplift could be applicable\nL72: if Ford Otosan's SCP weakens, if FMC guarantees bulk of its debt, or if FMC's ratings were upgraded.\nL73: https://www.fitchratings.com/research/corporate-finance/fitch-publishes-ford-otosan-bb-idr-outlook-stable-rates-proposed-eurobond-bb-exp-11-04-2024\nL74: 3/13\nL75: 4/11/24, 10:51 AM\nL76: Fitch Publishes Ford Otosan's 'BB+' IDR; Outlook Stable; Rates Proposed Eurobond 'BB+(EXP)'\nL77: Country Ceiling Not Limiting Factor: Ford Otosan's IDR is not limited by a Country Ceiling because we apply the Romanian Country\nL78: Ceiling of 'BBB+', reflecting its multi-country operations instead of Turkiye (B+) where the issuer is legally based. This stems from our\nL79: estimate that Romania-originated EBITDA in euros from the contract manufacturing agreement with FMC (euro-denominated export\nL80: sales) is more than sufficient to cover the HC (euro and US dollar) interest expenses.\nL81: DERIVATION SUMMARY\nL82: Ford Otosan's modest business profile is characterised by its comparatively smaller size that does not match higher-rated original\nL83: equipment manufacturers (OEM) like FMC, or Renault SA, which have higher production volumes driven by their sizeable passenger car\nL84: production capacities. Nevertheless, mirrored by FMC's leading CV market shares in Europe, Ford Otosan is a sizeable light/medium CV\nL85: producer in Europe, with production capacity that matches or surpasses peers like Mercedes Vans or Renault CVs.\nL86: Ford Otosan's heavy/truck manufacturing capacity is smaller than truck manufacturers like AB Volvo (NR) and Iveco Group N.V.\nL87: (BBB-/Stable). Ford Otosan's product diversification is also deemed limited, with the issuer's revenues dependent on a handful of Ford-\nL88: branded models. Our forecast revenues for Ford Otosan are concentrated on four models including vans and Puma, which is one of the\nL89: main reasons its business profile is constrained below investment-grade medians in our rating criteria for auto manufacturers.\nL90: The issuer has some geographic concentration to European market, with no exposure to APAC or the US. Nevertheless, we do not deem\nL91: this as a major rating constraint. Its financial profile compares favorably with most investment-grade rated CV manufacturers and\nL92: passenger car OEMs. We forecast an EBIT margin of around 6% for Ford Otosan, which is at the 'A' rating median and similar to that of\nL93: FMC, and Volkswagen AG (A-/Stable).\nL94: The heavy investment cycle in new models will drive new debt issuance and push EBITDA net leverage to around 2x in the medium term,\nL95: which is higher than peers, and above investment-grade medians in our sector criteria. We do not expect EBITDA net leverage to be\nL96: below 1.0x in our four-year forecast period, which is the 'bb' rating median, flagging leverage as a constraint below investment-grade\nL97: ratings. As the new models launch, and deliveries begin, we expect Ford Otosan's capital structure to improve rapidly. Nevertheless, this\nL98: impact will be beyond our forecast period of four years.\nL99: KEY ASSUMPTIONS\nL100: https://www.fitchratings.com/research/corporate-finance/fitch-publishes-ford-otosan-bb-idr-outlook-stable-rates-proposed-eurobond-bb-exp-11-04-2024\nL101: 4/13\nL102: 4/11/24, 10:51 AM\nL103: Fitch Publishes Ford Otosan's 'BB+' IDR; Outlook Stable; Rates Proposed Eurobond 'BB+(EXP)'\nL104: Fitch's Key Assumptions Within Our Rating Case for the Issuer:\nL105: - Annual export unit sales reaching 700,000 by 2025\nL106: - EBITDA margins trending toward mid-single digit in 2027 from high single digit in 2024\nL107: - Capex in line with investment guarantee scheme\nL108: - 2024 net working capital change driven by inventory during model switch\nL109: - Successful placement of Eurobond\nL110: - Dividend pay-out ratio at 50%\nL111: RATING SENSITIVITIES\nL112: Factors that Could, Individually or Collectively, Lead to Positive Rating Action/Upgrade:\nL113: - An upgrade of FMC or strengthening of legal incentives for FMC to support Ford Otosan\nL114: - Net cash position\nL115: - Through-the-cycle FCF margin sustained above 4%\nL116: Factors that Could, Individually or Collectively, Lead to Negative Rating Action/Downgrade:\nL117: - EBITDA margin sustainably below 6%\nL118: - Through-the-cycle FCF margin sustained below 1%\nL119: https://www.fitchratings.com/research/corporate-finance/fitch-publishes-ford-otosan-bb-idr-outlook-stable-rates-proposed-eurobond-bb-exp-11-04-2024\nL120: 5/13\nL121: 4/11/24, 10:51 AM\nL122: Fitch Publishes Ford Otosan's 'BB+' IDR; Outlook Stable; Rates Proposed Eurobond 'BB+(EXP)'\nL123: - EBITDA net leverage above 2.0x by 2025\nL124: - Multi-notch downgrade of FMC or adverse change to contractual sales to FMC\nL125: LIQUIDITY AND DEBT STRUCTURE\nL126: Comfortable Liquidity: Ford Otosan had TRY5.0 billion of available cash as of end-2023, after Fitch's adjustment for restricted cash.\nL127: Management targets maintaining a level of cash and credit commitments to meet 21 days of working capital outflows. The export\nL128: receivables relate to a single counterparty, which is Ford Europe. The average receivable collection time at Turkish and Romanian plants\nL129: are within 14 days and 30 days, respectively.\nL130: For domestic sales, a direct debit system is used for sales via dealers to mitigate credit risk. Ford Otosan uses letters of credit and trade\nL131: lines for export sales, and has a EUR100 million revolver for working capital needs. We expect negative FCF in the rating horizon\nL132: reflecting a capex spike and ongoing dividend payments.\nL133: Barbell Debt Maturity Profile Expected: Otosan's debt structure comprises both term loan A and term loan Bs. Most term loan Bs and\nL134: the planned Eurobond are expected to have maturity between 2026 and 2028, reducing near-term liquidity needs after the planned\nL135: refinancing. The short-term debt is refinanced on a rolling basis each year.\nL136: ISSUER PROFILE\nL137: Ford Otosan is a Turkish automotive manufacturing company that is specialised in light vehicle production, with leading market shares\nL138: in Europe. The company is a joint venture between Koc Holding (41%) and Ford Motor Company (41%), rest of the shares are free float.\nL139: DATE OF RELEVANT COMMITTEE\nL140: 04 April 2024\nL141: REFERENCES FOR SUBSTANTIALLY MATERIAL SOURCE CITED AS KEY DRIVER OF RATING\nL142: The principal sources of information used in the analysis are described in the Applicable Criteria.\nL143: https://www.fitchratings.com/research/corporate-finance/fitch-publishes-ford-otosan-bb-idr-outlook-stable-rates-proposed-eurobond-bb-exp-11-04-2024\nL144: 6/13\nL145: 4/11/24, 10:51 AM\nL146: Fitch Publishes Ford Otosan's 'BB+' IDR; Outlook Stable; Rates Proposed Eurobond 'BB+(EXP)'\nL147: ESG CONSIDERATIONS\nL148: The highest level of ESG credit relevance is a score of '3', unless otherwise disclosed in this section. A score of '3' means ESG issues are\nL149: credit-neutral or have only a minimal credit impact on the entity, either due to their nature or the way in which they are being managed\nL150: by the entity. Fitch's ESG Relevance Scores are not inputs in the rating process; they are an observation on the relevance and materiality\nL151: of ESG factors 